gov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-19","committee":"Guildford and Waverley Joint Governance Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-23","committee":"Guildford and Waverley Joint Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-01","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee (New)","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-02","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Guildford and Waverley Joint Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Shareholder and Trustee Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Guildford Council forecasts \u00a31m overspend as environmental and community services face staffing pressures","lead":"Guildford Council is projecting to overspend its General Fund budget by \u00a31.066 million in 2026-27, the council's financial monitoring report shows. The overspend reflects cumulative pressures across Environmental Services, Community Services and Regulatory Services. Environmental Services is facing a \u00a3487,000 shortfall in Trade Waste income and \u00a3350,000 in agency staffing costs to cover vacant posts, while Community Services is \u00a375,000 over budget due to a delayed staffing restructure. The council has also approved a \u00a3200,000 supplementary estimate for unbudgeted IT costs, to be funded from reserves. The pressures come as the council manages a transition to West Surrey Council, due to take effect on 1 April 2027, and faces wider governance challenges including an ongoing forensic audit of Housing Repairs and Maintenance and an external audit disclaimer on its 2025-26 accounts.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Risk Management &amp; Corp Risk Registers (Q1 2026-27)","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45595/45595.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Guildford Council forecasts \u00a31m overspend as income and staffing costs slip","lead":"Guildford Borough Council is projecting a General Fund overspend of \u00a31.066 million for 2026\u201327, the council's Executive heard on 17 September. The shortfall reflects a combination of income failures and cost pressures across multiple services. Trade Waste income is underachieving by \u00a3487,000 against a budgeted \u00a31.857 million; agency and casual staffing costs in Environmental Services are running \u00a3350,000 over budget; a delayed staffing restructure in Community Services has added \u00a375,000 to costs; and the council is requesting a supplementary estimate of \u00a3200,000 for unbudgeted IT expenses, to be funded from reserves. Officers are monitoring the position and will review pressures during the year, but the forecast suggests the council's original budget materially underestimated both its spending and the income it would receive.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Risk Management &amp; Corp Risk Registers (Q1 2026-27)","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45595/45595.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Guildford Council forecasts \u00a31.066m overspend in day-to-day spending","lead":"Guildford Council is forecasting an overspend of \u00a31.066 million on its day-to-day spending for 2026-27. The council faces multiple pressures including a \u00a3487,000 shortfall in Trade Waste Services income, \u00a3350,000 in additional agency staffing costs to cover vacancies, and a \u00a375,000 cost from delays to a planned Community Services restructure. It is also funding \u00a3200,000 of unbudgeted IT costs from its reserves (money it has set aside). The overspend forecast comes as external auditors issued a disclaimer of opinion on the 2025-26 accounts, unable to fully verify the figures because the previous two years' accounts were unaudited. A forensic audit is currently underway into governance and fraud concerns in Housing Repairs and Maintenance.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Risk Management &amp; Corp Risk Registers (Q1 2026-27)","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45595/45595.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Appendix 2 - Quarter 1 - Variance Explanation","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45600/45600.pdf","headline":"Regulatory Services income miss of \u00a3199k from unmet credit targets","detail":"Regulatory Services is reporting an adverse variance of \u00a3199k, primarily attributed to forecast underachievement of what the council terms 'vacant credit'. The paper does not elaborate on the specific cause or nature of this income shortfall.","category":"income","matters":"Regulatory services income (planning fees, building control, licensing) is normally predictable. An income shortfall suggests either softening demand for services or collection difficulties, either of which could be structural.","figures":"\u00a3199,390 adverse variance","quote":"The adverse variance relates mainly to the forecasted underachievement of vacant credit.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Appendix 2 - Quarter 1 - Variance Explanation","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45600/45600.pdf","headline":"Community Services staffing restructure savings forecast not to materialise","detail":"Community Services reports a \u00a3276k adverse variance due to forecast non-achievement of planned staffing restructure savings. The council is pursuing a cross-council collaboration case for 2026-27, commencing with senior establishment posts, indicating the original efficiency plan cannot be delivered within this service alone.","category":"savings","matters":"Savings plans that do not land are a leading indicator of structural budget pressure. The shift to requiring cross-council collaboration suggests this service cannot control its baseline costs independently, risking future budget pressures.","figures":"\u00a3276,715 adverse variance","quote":"There is a forecast non-achievement of the staffing restructure saving. A case for collaboration is underway for 2026-27, commencing with a number of the senior establishment posts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Appendix 2 - Quarter 1 - Variance Explanation","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45600/45600.pdf","headline":"Environmental Services waste income \u00a31.362m below budget; council acknowledges targets were unrealistic","detail":"Environmental Services forecasts income of \u00a30.475m against a budgeted \u00a31.837m, representing a 74% shortfall. The council has reviewed these targets and explicitly concluded they are unrealistic within waste services and will be amended going forward. Additionally, the council reports unbudgeted agency costs and a vacancy credit budget not expected to be met, totalling \u00a30.385m.","category":"income","matters":"A 74% shortfall on budgeted waste income indicates either demand weakness or a pricing/collection problem, either of which could impair future revenue. The council's admission that targets are unrealistic raises questions about forecasting quality and the reliability of budget planning.","figures":"\u00a30.475 million against a budgeted income of \u00a31.837 million; unbudgeted agency costs and vacancy credit shortfall totalling \u00a30.385 million","quote":"There is a forecasted underachievement in income of \u00a30.475 million against a budgeted income of \u00a31.837 million. Following a review, these income targets are unrealistic within waste and will be amended going forward. Additionally, there are unbudgeted agency costs over and above vacant posts and a vacancy credit budget not expected to be met, both totalling \u00a30.385 million.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Guildford Strategic Risk Q1 register 26-27 Final","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45596/45596.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation creates unquantified financial risk to medium-term financial sustainability","detail":"The council is transitioning to West Surrey unitary authority. The council identifies 'potentially significant financial implications' from reorganisation that remain unquantified at this stage. These impacts include transition costs (staffing, advice, programme support) and structural financial changes arising from the merger of two authorities' positions, which may further constrain resources available for council priorities.","category":"other","matters":"A lender needs to understand whether unquantified reorganisation costs could exceed the council's current financial headroom and force difficult choices around service delivery, capital investment, or additional borrowing.","figures":"No quantified figures; reorganisation scheduled for Vesting Day (date not specified in this extract)","quote":"There are also potentially significant financial implications associated with the transition, which remain unquantified at this stage and may further constrain resources.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Guildford Strategic Risk Q1 register 26-27 Final","paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45596/45596.pdf","headline":"Council forecasts \u00a31.709m revenue budget gap by 2029/30 despite currently balanced budget","detail":"While the Medium-Term Financial Plan shows a balanced budget through 2027/28, supported by strengthened reserves, a forecast revenue budget gap of \u00a31.709m (approximately 10% of the council's \u00a317m net revenue budget) emerges by 2029/30. The council identifies future pressures and savings requirements to close this gap, reflecting anticipated funding constraints from central government.","category":"savings","matters":"A lender needs to understand whether the council can achieve the savings necessary to bridge the gap without service reductions that might affect its financial stability or ability to service borrowing.","figures":"Forecast budget gap of \u00a31.709m by 2029/30; 2026/27 net revenue budget approximately \u00a317 million","quote":"The MTFP identifies future pressures and savings requirements, with a forecast budget gap of \u00a31.709m by 2029/30.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Guildford","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Regulatory Services income miss of \u00a3199k from unmet credit targets","lead":"Regulatory services income (planning fees, building control, licensing) is normally predictable. An income shortfall suggests either softening demand for services or collection difficulties, either of which could be structural.","also":["53 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Guildford & Waverley Joint Executive Committee on 2026-10-06","n_findings":54,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 2 - Quarter 1 - Variance Explanation","top_paper_url":"https://guildford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s45600/45600.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":98,"news_drafts_total":3}}